---
// Core page. Copy authored against briefs/maltipoo-size.json (2026-07-13).
// Toy-first sizing owner-confirmed; kg-first for the local audience.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AnswerCapsule from '../components/AnswerCapsule.astro';
import CaptionedBlock from '../components/CaptionedBlock.astro';
import FactsCard from '../components/FactsCard.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CtaClose from '../components/CtaClose.astro';
import { site, whatsappLink } from '../data/site';
import { webPageSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'Maltipoo Full Grown Size: How Big Do They Get | Maltipoo.sg';
const description =
  'A toy Maltipoo finishes 2 to 4kg full grown; minis run larger. Growth timeline in kg, when they stop growing, and what the toy, mini and teacup labels really mean in Singapore.';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Size Guide', url: '/maltipoo-size' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'When does Maltipoo stop growing?',
    a: 'Most Maltipoos reach near-adult size between 8 and 12 months, with the frame finishing before the coat does. The fastest visible growth happens around months 3 to 5, which is why a 4-month-old already looks close to its adult outline.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How can I predict my puppy’s adult size?',
    a: 'Look at the parents, not the label on the listing. A puppy from a 3kg Maltese and a 3kg Toy Poodle will finish in that neighbourhood. We show you the parents’ sizing in person, which beats any size chart or marketing term.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a teacup Maltipoo real?',
    a: 'Teacup is a marketing label, not a recognised size class, and a teacup Maltipoo full grown often lands where a normal toy does anyway. The label usually means an unusually small puppy at a premium, with health risks from extreme miniaturisation. A healthy 2 to 4kg toy is already small enough for any Singapore home.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are male Maltipoos bigger than females?',
    a: 'Slightly, on average, but within the toy range the difference is a few hundred grams, not a size class. Parent sizing and the individual puppy matter far more than gender.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How tall is a full grown Maltipoo?',
    a: 'Toys stand roughly 20 to 30cm at the shoulder. [VERIFY range against a vet source before launch] Height varies with the Poodle parent more than the Maltese side.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does size change the price?',
    a: 'Yes: within our $3,288 to $5,988 all-in range, a smaller expected adult frame sits higher. The price guide explains both drivers; the label on a listing should never be one of them.',
  },
];

const schemas = [
  webPageSchema({
    path: '/maltipoo-size',
    title,
    description,
    aboutName: 'Maltipoo full grown size',
    datePublished: '2026-07-13',
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  faqSchema(faqItems),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/maltipoo-size" schemas={schemas}>
  <section class="container-site pt-4">
    <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-panel)] bg-tint-200 px-6 py-10 md:px-12">
      <Breadcrumb items={crumbs} bare class="mb-5" />
      <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Size guide</p>
      <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.9rem,4vw,2.8rem)] mb-3 max-w-[24ch]">How big do Maltipoos get?</h1>
      <p class="text-ink-soft max-w-[52ch] mb-5">
        Most maltipoo sizes chart pages online are American and speak in pounds. Here is the
        maltipoo weight kg answer instead, from the sizes we actually place in Singapore.
      </p>
      <a href={whatsappLink('Hi! Could you share the expected adult size of your available Maltipoo puppies?')} class="btn-cta">Ask about sizes</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <AnswerCapsule question="What is a Maltipoo's full grown size?">
    A toy Maltipoo is 2 to 4kg full grown, and that is the maltipoo full grown size for most
    of the puppies we place in Singapore; minis from a larger Poodle parent run roughly 4 to
    6kg. Most reach near-adult size by 8 to 12 months. "Teacup" is a marketing label, not a
    size class.
  </AnswerCapsule>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="The growth timeline, month by month" id="timeline">
    <p>
      At homecoming, around week nine, a toy Maltipoo weighs a fraction of its adult figure
      and fits in two palms. [VERIFY typical homecoming weight with owner] The visible growth
      spurt runs roughly months three to five, which is why a maltipoo 4 months old already
      shows its adult outline, and by 8 to 12 months the frame is essentially done.
    </p>
    <p>
      The coat finishes later than the skeleton: puppy fluff gives way to the adult coat
      through the first year, often lightening in shade as it goes. So judge adult size by the
      frame and the parents, and judge the adult coat with patience.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Growth stages, kg-first"
      items={[
        'Week 9: homecoming, palm-sized',
        'Months 3 to 5: the visible growth spurt',
        'Months 8 to 12: near-adult frame',
        'Toy adult: 2 to 4kg; mini adult: 4 to 6kg',
        'Coat matures after the frame, often lighter',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Toy, mini, teacup: decoding the labels" id="labels" reverse>
    <p>
      Toy is what we place: a Maltese crossed with a Toy Poodle, and a toy Maltipoo full
      grown finishes 2 to 4kg, the maltipoo adult size buyers here should picture. Mini
      means a Miniature Poodle parent and a 4 to 6kg adult. Teacup means marketing: an
      unusually small puppy, a premium price, and health risks that arrive later, which is why
      the label never appears on our cards or in the
      <a href="/maltipoo-price-singapore">price guide</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A note from the shop floor: buyers arrive quoting labels from overseas breeder sites,
      and the labels predict adult size worse than a thirty-second look at the parents. We
      keep the parents' sizing on record for every litter and show you in person; that is the
      whole trick.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="The label decoder"
      items={[
        'Toy: Toy Poodle parent, 2 to 4kg adult, what we place',
        'Mini: Miniature Poodle parent, 4 to 6kg adult',
        'Teacup: a price label, not a size class',
        'Best predictor: the parents, every time',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="What 2 to 4kg means day to day in Singapore" id="day-to-day" tinted>
    <p>
      A featherweight dog reshapes the practical stuff. It rides in a carrier on the MRT and
      in a Grab without negotiation, sits in the smallest grooming price tier ($50 to $70 for
      a basic groom), eats a small bag of food slowly, and takes up one sofa cushion in any
      <a href="/maltipoo-hdb-approved">HDB flat</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The one honest caution: 2kg of dog is delicate. Toddlers who hug hard and staircases
      without supervision are the real risks at this size, so homes with very young children
      should handle-train the humans before the puppy arrives.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Featherweight, practically"
      items={[
        'Carrier-friendly on MRT and Grab',
        'Smallest grooming price tier',
        'Tiny food bills',
        'Delicate with toddlers: supervise hugs',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />

  <CtaClose
    secondaryHeading="Meet the parents"
    secondaryText="The honest size answer is standing in our shop. See who is available, then come look."
    secondaryHref="/puppies"
    secondaryLabel="See available puppies"
  />
</BaseLayout>
